<description>
Rebuild the visible frame fragments as centered concentric square rings.

Input structure
The input has a dominant background and several non-background colors. Each
foreground color marks one square frame or center block, possibly with gaps
caused by overlap with other frames. The intended side length of a color is
the larger side of the bbox of all pixels of that color.

Rule
Sort the colors by intended side length from largest to smallest. Create a
square output whose side is the largest length, then draw each color as the
border of the centered square with that side length.

Key insight
Positions in the input are only a scrambled/overlapped display of the
nested frames; the bbox side lengths provide the layer order and output
ring sizes.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "foreground color masks s, each with side length max bbox height/width, ordered largest to smallest",
  "dst": "centered square frame layers d ordered from outermost to innermost",
  "match_cond": "rank(s by decreasing side length) == rank(d from outside inward)",
  "transform": "draw each s.color as the one-cell border of its matched centered square",
  "grid_shape": "square side length is the largest source bbox side length"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task_type: move_in_order
  grid:
    input: square, 13-19 rows/cols; bg=0
    output: square NxN where N = largest frame size
  frames:
    count: 4-5 distinct foreground colors
    sizes: N, N-2, N-4, ..., 1 or 2 (consecutive odd or even series); N in 8-10
    shape: each is a 1-cell-wide square frame border (SxS), or a single pixel if S=1
    colors: all distinct, no reuse, none equals bg
  input_placement:
    each frame drawn at an arbitrary position on the grid as its SxS border
    frames may partially overlap; later-drawn frames occlude earlier ones
    frames may extend partially off-grid (clipped) [observed, 1 of 3 examples]
    at least 1 pair of frames has overlapping bboxes; 1-3 pairs overlap
    non-overlapping frames separated by >=1 bg cell [observed]
    all visible pixels of a frame lie on the border of its SxS bbox
  output_rule: |
    sort frames by size descending; create NxN grid; draw each as a centered
    concentric ring (outermost = largest frame, innermost = smallest)
generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. pick N from 8-10; compute sizes = [N, N-2, ..., 1 or 2]; assign a unique color to each
    2. build the NxN output with concentric frames (validates ring structure)
    3. choose input side S = N + randint(4, 9) (ensuring S <= 30)
    4. for each frame (size, color), pick a random top-left (r, c) on the SxS input grid
       such that at least 1 row and 1 col of the frame is visible; allow partial off-grid
    5. ensure at least 1 pair of frame bboxes overlap
    6. draw frames onto the input in a random order; later draws overwrite earlier pixels
    7. verify each color's max(bbox_h, bbox_w) still equals its intended size
       (if occluded color lost its max dimension, re-pick positions and retry)
